WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:03.580
Flu season in the United States and Europe

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.310
is approaching.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.080
Many people will roll up their sleeves for the jab.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.660
Some will be protected.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.040
Some will suffer.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.820
Sure, the numbers are pretty good --

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.880
at best about 60 percent of people who get the shot

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.140
will dodge the flu,

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:21.020
at worst only 10 percent will be protected.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.900
The flu virus constantly mutates,

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.060
which forces vaccine manufacturers

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.720
to reformulate it every year.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.920
It takes about 7 months to make the vaccine

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.120
so different strains pop up by the time it is ready

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.020
which is the long standing explanation

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.520
of why the vaccine often fails.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.480
But it’s more complicated than that.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.970
And something can be done about it.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.100
Why doesn’t the flu vaccine always work?

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.320
Eggs

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.580
The form of the virus used in the flu vaccine

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.220
is actually grown inside of chicken eggs.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.180
Researchers discovered that mutations

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:55.240
in the virus occur during this growth--

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.720
often in regions important

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:57.900
to triggering immune responses--

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.660
and this can undermine the vaccine’s ability

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.690
to protect people.

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:03.440
Ferrets

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.040
Ferrets are the most popular

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.500
animal model for human influenza.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.220
The traditional way to select a strain for the vaccine

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.100
involves injecting ferrets with an influenza virus

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.960
used to make a vaccine strain,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.880
harvesting the antibodies,

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.420
and then mixing them with a virus

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.020
isolated from a human who recently had influenza.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.680
If the antibodies don’t stop the virus,

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.860
the vaccine strain is changed.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.420
Aside from the fact that ferrets aren’t humans,

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.340
antibodies aren’t viral sequences.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.880
Ghosts of flus past

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.120
We repeatedly get flu vaccines

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.219
and are naturally exposed to the virus in the wild.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.320
And it turns out that our first exposure in our lives

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.800
oddly sets the table for all flu reactions after.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.440
This dizzyingly complex history

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.840
makes predicting the impact of a flu

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.439
vaccine in an individual staggeringly difficult.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.040
OK, so what can we do about it?

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.960
Stop growing it in eggs

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.740
and start using genetically engineered viral proteins

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.380
Stop relying so heavily on ferrets

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:12.240
and more purposefully factor in viral sequence data

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.000
Learn more about the immune responses

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.380
linked to protection

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:18.700
and how to outwit the original exposure

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.540
Incorporate parts of the virus that rarely mutate,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.240
ideally creating a “universal” shot

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.160
that targets all strains and lasts for a lifetime

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.420
But for now, experts say the flu vaccine,

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.400
even given its limits, can still save lives

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.400
and spare suffering for many people,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.340
especially anyone with a compromised immune system

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.840
and the elderly.

